/*
 * EXAMPLE 14 — OrderService, singular case.
 *
 * Ten widgets are stocked, four are ordered, the order is shipped, and the shelf
 * is counted again.  The quantities are chosen so that every number in the run is
 * distinct: 10 in, 4 out, 6 left, one order, two audit events.  A fault that
 * confused any of those counters for another shows up as a different number
 * rather than as a coincidence.
 *
 * orderId is NOT bound here.  It is the ledger's to choose — Helper does not even
 * see it until Ledger.open returns — so it is captured from placeOrder and
 * threaded into ship by the propagation scan.  That the value crosses a class
 * boundary on the way changes nothing about how it is threaded, which is the
 * point: propagation is a property of the specification, not of the library's
 * internal structure.
 */
test OrderServiceDryRun {
    sequence: restock -> placeOrder -> ship -> stockLevel;

    inputs {
        restock[0].sku = "widget";
        restock[0].quantity = 10;
        placeOrder[1].sku = "widget";
        placeOrder[1].quantity = 4;
        stockLevel[3].sku = "widget";
    }
}
